A||||1||Krashdavus||Cold Start Squeal Noise||||5||
Z||000000||Krashdavus||11-22-2001||02:22 PM||||My '88 GT has a cold start squeal that can last about 3-7 seconds, then it goes away.  It's not repeatable on a warm start.  The alternator is new, but I don't know how old the belts are (we just bought the car back in May of this year), it has almost 50K mi. on it.<P>In addition, it has an extremely high idle on it - 1500 rpm, until it warms up, and I'm mean really warms up 170F or more.  Once it warms up the idle reduces to 1000.  It's a 5 speed.  I've checked the base timing, and it'd dead on.<P>MAP is new, temperature switches are new.  IAT is dead on.  IAC counts look good, about 25.  My assumptionis a minor intake vaccuum leak that seals itself as the engine warms up..any thoughts???||||reg||5
Z||000001||reddog||11-22-2001||06:02 PM||||If your alternator belt is already tight try starting the motor and letting it idle while placing a bar of soap against the underside of the belt for a few seconds. Fiero's have a tendency to squeal no matter how many times you tighten the belts. I was skeptical about the soap trick until I tried everything else and failed the soap really worked for mine.As for the high idle it could be a small crack in your egr tube or an intake leak but not likely other wise it would have a very noticeably surge or hunting idle, try turning your idle screw to the left a little and see if that helps! Although 1000rpm isn't that far from correct. Have you got performance chip in your car?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
